<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gihon Spring</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gihon Spring is the main natural spring of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, in the Kidron valley below the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. Its water made settlement on the ridge possible, and massive towers were built to protect it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> dug the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel">Siloam tunnel</a> in the late 8th century BCE to carry its water inside the city walls, and the Bible names it as the place where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> was anointed king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gihon-spring.jpg" alt="Gihon Spring" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Roberts · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D7%9E%D7%A2%D7%99%D7%99%D7%9F_%D7%94%D7%92%D7%99%D7%97%D7%95%D7%9F_%D7%A8%D7%95%D7%91%D7%A8%D7%98%D7%A1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Hezekiah rebelled against Assyria, he strengthened Jerusalem with a broad wall and dug the Siloam tunnel to bring water from the <b>Gihon</b> spring inside the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Jerusalem grew around the <b>Gihon</b> spring on a ridge in the Judaean hills.</blockquote><a href="/state/urusalim">Urusalim</a></li></ul></section>
